ut what they should or  shouldn't do  and that stems right back to last summer  when dominic cummings as the prime  minister's senior advisor  got away with run it driving all the way  up to  county durham and back again breaching  the rules  and with no sanction at all in fact he  got a pretty big pay rise after that  from  uh from what i heard now the  government's constant copying and uh  chopping and changing one rule for them  another rule for everybody else simply  confuses everybody even in the  you know the period in the run-up to  christmas we had matt hancock  standing in the chamber of the house of  commons telling us that there was a new  variant  of the disease uh spreading in kent and  parts of  east london that was more contagious two  days after  that boris johnson stood up in the house  of commons and told us  it was perfectly safe to ease the  restrictions for five days  over christmas we were all wondering  whether these two senior  conservative politicians actually speak  to each other and of course in the end  boris johnson had to pull back from that  position and we got one day instead of  the  full five but the chopping and changing  the inconsistency  really confuses people and given the  crisis we're seeing now in our hospitals  up and down  the country we need the government to  really focus  on getting this message crystal clear  the public need to know  the police need to know we all need to  know just stay at home  as much as you can and that should be  what the government focuses on it's  interesting you know  asking you questions about fines that  are happening now about compliance about  what the police should be doing and what  i don't want to  diminish the importance of what happened  with dominic cummings you know you refer  all the way back to something that  happened  like way back in last year i mean some  people might think that's just playing  politics  well it isn't if you if you speak to  people and i speak to an awful lot of  people about that  that is people just using it as an  excuse though i mean look we all know  what we should be doing now don't we  we should be staying at home like you  say we should be following the rules  trust in what the government was saying  broke you know the government broke that  trust  and they should be doing things to fix  that broken trust but the constant  chopping and changing  i just gave you know the example before  christmas  uh matt hancock telling us there was a  more virulent strain of the virus  more contagious on the monday on the  wednesday boris johnson's saying that  was going to have no impact on five days  of relaxation of the rules over  christmas and then just a few days later  you turned again now if the government  knew that information about the virus  and i presume matt hancock and boris  johnson do speak to each other  they should have been crystal clear from  then that we couldn't have five days  relaxation over christmas  it's consistency of message that we need  not chopping  and changing of of course you know the  consistency of messages  absolutely you know vital is really  important but  i do sometimes wonder it's quite easy to  criticize the government isn't it does  labor need to show a bit more  leadership too about a way out about  things that should be  done in the future rather than looking  back to the past  and in an interview with my colleague  sam coats this week for example kirsten  said  it's inevitable that schools will have  to close and therefore they need to be  part of the national restrictions  package  that needs to come into place i mean  it's not really leadership to call for  things that are already inevitable is it  well the thing as you you just said  sophie and you're absolutely right the  thing to focus on now  is moving forwards what's happened has  happened  the way out of this crisis now is is  getting the vaccinations out  across the country at pace now we are  pushing the government  to hit the target of 2 million  vaccinations  a week we have to absolutely move heaven  and earth to get those vaccinations out  now  i understand there are issues with  supply of the vaccine at the moment it  would help  if the government would give a daily  briefing or they told us how much  supply of the vaccine is available how  much of the vaccine has actually been  put into  people's arms and in every part of the  country so that we can scrutinize  the situation and see what's going on  the second point here  is the government needs to start  planning now for not just hitting the  two million a week target but exceeding  it  because we need to get this vaccine out  as quickly as possible because that is  the only way  that we can end this uh this nightmare  that we're in uh as a country at the  moment now we've  we've heard this week haven't we from  pharmacists who are trained  to give injections have not been called  on to help with uh  with delivering the vaccine when it  happens i've had dentists in my  constituency telling me the same thing  i've had local government leaders  telling me they are offering the  government  buildings spaces where vaccines can be  delivered help to  manage volunteers who can go out and  reach the harder to reach  parts of the population the government  hasn't responded to them you know  the government said no one will have to  travel more than 10 miles to get a  vaccine well actually 10 miles is a long  way  to go if you don't if you don't have a  car  there are buildings that communities own  all the councils own in every village  town suburb  of this country that could be made  available i'm standing  okay you see it because of the fault but  just behind me there's a building here  that could be available for this  community why the government needs to  start planning now to get this vaccine  out at pace you know thanks to our  scientists  we were the first country in the world  to get the vaccine let's now set  ourselves the ambition to be the first  country in the world to be vaccinated  and move on from this crisis okay um  interesting that you just called for  more data on vaccines that i think we're  expecting from monday so  you know handy that labour's calling for  something that we are expecting  to see if they give what they've said  they're going to give you know i  i hope they do give us full transparency  because in too many other cases  so far they haven't okay um matt hancock  said earlier as well that if  you're a key worker and you possibly can  keep your child at school  at home sorry rather than sending them  to school so if just one of you as a key  worker for example  then that's what parents should do do  you think that actually parents do need  to be taking a bit more responsibility  and only  sending children to school if they need  to  well i think what ma i heard what matt  hancock uh said on that and i think on  that point he was  uh he was right you know if if parents  even key workers can keep their children  at home rather than sending them to  school then that is what  uh they need to do but we need to be  absolutely  clear here as well that the schools are  open for the children of key workers  because we need those key workers  whether they're social workers health  workers care workers we need them out  there doing their jobs to keep  people safe so the schools are open for  the children of key workers  um but if like everybody else if there  are key workers who are able  safely to work from home and they can  have their children with them then  that's what they should do  okay and and just finally you're calling  for the government to scrap  a planned rise in council tax at the  same time of course the economy is  obviously  in a pretty dire situation because of  kv-19  don't taxes need to rise  well what the government said at the  start of the last lockdown was that they  would  give whatever support councils needed to  support communities to get through 